I applaud your desire and drive.   You will find that VMS is a very
different world from PCs and Unix/Linux system.   Some things that
you take for granted are not there (some good, some bad), but VMS
has many features that are far beyond what others offer, particularly
if you are trying to build truly reliable systems.

As for your belief that nothing is beyond you, you are correct.
Years ago, the company I worked for hired a gentleman (hi, JK) for
a contract job to write an RSX-11M device driver.   Normally, I would
have been the one to do it, but I was busy with another one.

For the first few weeks his progress seemed a bit slow, but we
attributed that to the somewhat ambiguous documentation for the
device and our requirements.   In the end, however, he produced a
driver that worked properly, filled our needs, and was well-documented.

Towards the end of the job, he confessed that this was his *first*
RSX-11M device driver!   Besides spending all day on the job, he
was spending every night studying the manuals and other device
drivers, and getting very little sleep.   His drive to succeed
was so strong that he crammed months of study into a few weeks.
I would hire someone like him in a flash over someone who had
more experience, but was less motivated.

If you have an open mind and the desire to learn, you will find
that VMS is a pleasure to use and program for.   One of its best
features is the documentation.   Besides the printed manuals
(about six feet of shelf space), it is also available on CDROM
and online at http://h71000.www7.hp.com/doc/os731_index.html

Also, *always* remember the HELP command!   It is far beyond
the Unix "man" pages.   Not only does it explain the purpose
and syntax for every command, but gives useful examples.

The "freeware" CDROMs are a valuable source of examples to learn
from.   They are included with VMS distributions, and are available
for sale or download (someone help me here with URLs, please).

Another good source of information is http://www.openvms.org/

If you want to get experience without the chance of screwing
up your employer's system, you can buy a cheap VAX or Alpha
on eBay and get a perfectly legal (and free) license to run
VMS at home.   It is called the VMS Hobbyist program and you
can find out more at http://www.openvmshobbyist.com/

Good luck,
Alan



