The Alpha is just a microprocessor architecture: a 64 bit RISC processor.
Between 1992 and today it was sold in four versions or "evolutions": from
EV4 to EV7. EV8 was on the drawing board but killed by HP. The EV56 is an
evolution 5 cpu design manufactured in the next generation facility.
Advantage: smaller die, faster clock.
When the Alpha hit the market it was the fastest cpu on the planet and it
held that title for several years. Depending on how you count or measure it
still holds that title.

The Alpha was designed to run multiple operating systems. It supports
openVMS, TRU64 unix and it supports Windows NT4. Windows 2000 came as far as
the beta release and was (is?) even available on the Internet. Various
flavors of Linux run on certain Alpha's. HP still supports RedHat
developments for the Alpha AFAIK.

The design effort for EV8 was recently killed by HP (aka "alphacide") in
favor of the Intel Itanium 64 bit processor.

VMS is supported on three platforms: the 32 bit VAX (1978-2002) the 64 bit
Alpha (1992-2007) and the 64 bit Itanium (2003 onwards).

The Alpha motherboard is similar in set up as any Intel 32 mobo. It uses
regular memory (though the earlier Alpha's used proprietary memory stacks,
available only at a stiff price) and PCI slots. Not all interfaces are
supported by VMS, so be careful be fore you buy a SCSI controler or network
interface off the shelf. Check the options catalog. The disks and tapes are
less of a problem, most current drives are supported by VMS.

Main advantages of VMS platforms:
- availability: booting a VMS system may be so rare that system managers
forget the procedure
- clustering: applications may run on various cluster members at the same
time, simultaneously accessing the same disk(s).
The latter concept might look similar to what NFS does, but a good deal more
reliable and a lot more functionality.

VMS also runs on a fourth platform: the VAX simulator. Examples are simh
(freeware) and Charon (priced product). Both simh and Charon run on Windows,
linux/unix platforms, sometimes at higher speeds than a real VAX ever could
achieve. But a simulator is not quite the real thing.




