> >  supported it on 11/780, but I've never actually seen one.
> 
> Google revealed two snippets, one that LAT arrived coincident with Decnet
> Phase IV, and the other that Phase IV arrived in 1982.
> 
> What was a typical networking interconnect in the early 80s?  I know I used
> a networked 780 and a couple of 11/34s in 81/82, but never really stopped to
> think what was physically connecting them...

In our case, lots of RS232 wiring to lots of DZ-11, and later DMZ-32 serial
interfaces. We stopped using this and put in ethernet and terminal servers
to eliminate the downtime we had each time there was a power disturbance
(cuts, or lightning strikes within about 5 miles). This problem was
eventually traced to the earth mat under the main building drying out in the
summer. In the autumn, when it rained, the problems went away.

The next entertainment was watching the pretty blue sparks when the shell of
the AUI cable touched the backplane of the VAX. Turned out that the "clean
earth" used for the ethernet cable was about 60V RMS away from the real
earth (used for the hardware).

Even with the sparks, it all worked.

Alan

