  True.  Some of the newer disks are cooler.  Some are not.  I know of 
  various folks that have baked their 7200 RPM spindles as a result of
  insufficient cooling available within the (often older) enclosure. 

  There was a large-scale up-rating of thermal (fan) cooling for some
  the StorageWorks shelves some years back when then-newer (and hotter)
  spindles were coming on-line, and it would not surprise me to learn
  that hobbyists are now acquiring these older (and hotter) spindles.

  I upgraded the fans for several BA356 shelves that were in use back
  during that time -- I'm still using two of those shelves and the same
  disks.  Some of the other local folks that did not upgrade to the
  then-new two-speed fans back then when they added in the then-newer
  and hotter disk spindles are, um, no longer using those disks. 

  The recent StorageWorks shelves rather substantial cooling abilities
  and the metal cooling fins are not strictly for decoration, after all.


