:Paul Repacholi <prep@prep.synonet.com> writes:
:> With a HSC50, you will not be able to run large packets and the new
:> arbitration scheme, so be sure that the CIPCA is set to the old
:> standard. It has probably been set to the newer protocol stuff. It is
:> in the good book.  I think you will also need to be sure the HSC50
:> Link module is up to rev for a CIPCA.
:
:The docs claim a CIPCA won't work with an HSC50 at all.

  We generally do not describe why configurations are not supported,
  whether due to a lack of testing, or due to one or more known problems,
  or any of various other potential decision cases.

  Per a recent hallway discussion with the CI Architect, there are good
  reasons why this configuration is considered unsupported.

  Can it be gotten to work?  Likely yes, though the configuration is
  exceedingly unlikely perform as expected if newer packet traffic is
  ever seen on the CI.  (OpenVMS Engineering decided to trade off the
  time and effort that would have been required to test and to explain
  how to properly configure a CIPCA with an older HSC50 and to then
  subsequently test and support the storage configuration with the
  engineering work needed for much newer and faster storage controllers
  and configurations -- we figured folks would have and want newer
  controllers, and would want newer controllers to be faster and more
  capable.  Given most HSC50 controllers are -- or were -- running 
  mid-1980's vintage DSA-class RA-series disks and TA-series tapes...)

  As for your other questions, CI port drivers are large undertakings.

  CI adapters are large hardware undertakings.

  I do not know that the CIPCA host specs were ever published -- given
  that various of the CI controllers know how to read host page tables,
  this effort can get interesting, too.

  For host-to-host communications, I would obviously use a LAN controller.

