Possibly one should recognize that DEC stopped making disk drives
several years ago.  The technology has advanced.

The first really good small DEC drive I had was a DSP3105.  I'm sure
there is an equivalent RZ number but I don't know it.  Checking back,
one finds capacity, rotation rate, average seek time, power dissipation:
DEC DSP3105              1GB  5400rpm 9.5ms 11.2W

Looking at some contemporary drives, one finds e.g.
Seagate  ST39216N        9GB  7200rpm 5.9ms  7.0W
Seagate ST318418N       18GB  7200rpm 6.9ms  7.6W
Seagate ST336918N       36GB  7200rpm 8.5ms  7.6W

The only reason that I picked Seagate for this table is that their
web site is easy to search.

So put new disk drives in your old StorageWorks SBBs.  Of course,
it is easier if the drives and the SBBs are SCA Single Connector
Attachment rather than 50-pin narrow as all my examples above were.

