I wrote:
> The docs claim a CIPCA won't work with an HSC50 at all.

hoff@hp.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
>   Can it be gotten to work?  Likely yes, though the configuration is
>   exceedingly unlikely perform as expected if newer packet traffic is
>   ever seen on the CI.

I won't have that problem.  The only devices I'll have on the CI are the
CIPCA, the CI20 (in a DECSYSTEM-2065), and a HSC50 (if I can get my
hands on one).  Of those, the CIPCA is the only device that supports
"newer packet traffic".

And since I'll be writing all the software to handle the CIPCA myself,
I should be able to keep it from generating "newer packet traffic".
(Unless the CIPCA on-board firmware does it behind my back.)

>   CI adapters are large hardware undertakings.

They were back then, but I'm not convinced that they would be today.
I've studied the CI port adapter portion of the CI20 print set
extensively, and it doesn't seem all that complicated.  (By current
standards, back in 1981 it was f*&$ing amazing).  It should easily fit
in a small FPGA (XC2S30 or larger) with a little bit of circuitry for the
PHYs.

>   I do not know that the CIPCA host specs were ever published -- given
>   that various of the CI controllers know how to read host page tables,
>   this effort can get interesting, too.

If the CIPCA firmware reads host page tables, it's of no use to me, since
I plan to use it on an x86 running Linux.  More reason for me to just
build my own CI adapter, I suppose.

>   For host-to-host communications, I would obviously use a LAN controller.

Good idea, except that DECSYSTEM-20 clustering doesn't work over a LAN.

Thanks,
Eric


